This is part four of [A History of Coffee](/coffee/guides/coffee-history/). Coffee had reached Europe through Venice ([part three](/coffee/guides/coffee-history-europe/)), but England took to coffeehouses with more enthusiasm than almost anywhere. For about 50 years, they were the center of London life.

## First mentions

English travelers to the Ottoman Empire wrote about coffee in the early 1600s. Captain **John Smith** (the same one from Jamestown) mentioned "coffa" from his time in Turkey around 1603, and the poet **George Sandys**, who traveled there in 1610, described Turks sitting in coffeehouses all day sipping a drink "black as soot, and tasting not much unlike it."

Not a great review, but people were curious.

## Oxford, 1637 and 1650

The diarist John Evelyn recorded that in **1637** a Greek student at Balliol College, Oxford, **Nathaniel Conopios**, made coffee for himself. It's one of the earliest records of anyone drinking coffee in England.

In **1650**, a man the antiquarian Anthony Wood called simply "Jacob a Jew" opened England's first coffeehouse at the Angel in Oxford. Later writers call him Lebanese or Turkish, and even the year is a little shaky, since Wood's own accounts don't quite agree. A few years later, Oxford's coffeehouses were where many of the scientists who went on to found the Royal Society met to talk.

## London, 1652: Pasqua Rosée

London's first coffeehouse came about because of a merchant named **Daniel Edwards**, who had picked up the coffee habit while trading in Turkey. He brought home his servant **Pasqua Rosée**, an Ottoman Greek, who made it for him every morning. So many friends came by for a cup that Edwards set Rosée up in business.

In **1652**, Rosée opened a coffee stall in **St Michael's Alley, Cornhill**, in the City of London. He handed out a leaflet called _The Vertue of the Coffee Drink_, which claimed coffee helped with everything from sore eyes to gout. A pub called the Jamaica Wine House stands on the site now, with a plaque.

## The coffeehouse boom

Coffeehouses spread fast through London and then other towns. A few things made them special:

- **A penny to get in.** Entry usually cost a penny, and the coffee was extra. For that you could stay as long as you liked, read the newspapers and pamphlets lying around, and talk to anyone. That's where the nickname **"penny universities"** came from.
- **Anyone could sit anywhere.** In a society obsessed with rank, coffeehouse rules said a gentleman and a tradesman shared the same tables. A poem posted in some coffeehouses in 1674 laid out the rules: no swearing, no quarrels, no gambling, and no one had to give up his seat to a "finer" man.
- **Sober conversation.** Taverns served beer and wine. Coffeehouses offered a place to talk business or politics with a clear head.
- **News.** Before most people could get a newspaper delivered, the coffeehouse was where you went to hear what was happening. Many had their own mail and gossip networks.

It was mostly men. Women ran some coffeehouses and served in many, but they weren't welcome as customers.

Different coffeehouses had different crowds. Writers went to **Will's**, where the poet John Dryden held court. Scientists from the Royal Society went to the **Grecian**. Later, in the early 1700s, magazines like _The Tatler_ and _The Spectator_ were written for, and partly from, coffeehouse conversation.

**How many were there?** It's often said that London had thousands by 1700. Estimates range from several hundred to about two thousand, and many historians now think the lower end is closer. Either way, there were a lot.

## The backlash

Not everyone was a fan.

- **1674:** _The Women's Petition Against Coffee_ complained that coffee made men lazy, talkative and useless at home. It was probably satire, written by men, and it got a reply, _The Men's Answer to the Women's Petition_, defending coffee.
- **1675:** **Charles II** saw coffeehouses as nests of political gossip and rebellion. On 29 December 1675, he issued a proclamation to shut them down. The outcry was immediate, from customers, coffeehouse owners and merchants (and from the government's own tax collectors, since coffee was taxed). On 8 January 1676, about eleven days later, the ban was withdrawn.

It was the same pattern as Mecca a century and a half earlier: ban coffee, regret it, back down.

## Where business got done

Coffeehouses became offices for whole industries, and some of those businesses are still around:

- **Lloyd's of London.** Edward Lloyd's coffeehouse, open by 1688, became the place where ship owners, captains and merchants met. Lloyd made it more useful by collecting shipping news, and people who were willing to insure ships and cargo started gathering there. Over the next century, that turned into **Lloyd's of London**, still one of the biggest insurance markets in the world.
- **The Stock Exchange.** Stockbrokers were known for being rowdy, and in the 1690s they were pushed out of the Royal Exchange. They moved into the coffeehouses of nearby Exchange Alley, especially **Jonathan's** and **Garraway's**, where they posted prices and made deals. In 1773, brokers renamed a new building "the Stock Exchange," the start of the London Stock Exchange.
- **Auctions.** Garraway's also hosted auctions "by the candle," where bidding stopped when a short candle burned out.

## By 1700

Coffee had gone from a curiosity to part of daily life in London in about 50 years. But in the same coffeehouses, a new drink was already for sale.

